# Commit after each change

This repo is set up so that **every logical agent change becomes its own
git commit**. The commits are checkpoints: they let the user roll back,
diff, or cherry-pick individual modifications without losing later work.

## When to commit

Commit at the end of each *logical* unit of work, before yielding the
turn back to the user. A logical unit is typically:

- One feature or bug fix the user asked for.
- One refactor that stands on its own.
- One configuration / tooling change (e.g. adding a rule, updating
  `CONTEXT.md`, bumping a dep).

If the user asks for several unrelated things in one message, make
several commits — one per thing — rather than a single mega-commit.

Do **not** commit:

- Half-finished work that doesn't type-check / build. Run `npx tsc
  --noEmit` and `cargo check --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml`
  first when the change touches the relevant side.
- Changes that only exist because you were exploring; revert them
  instead.
- Files that look like secrets (`.env`, credentials, tokens). Stop and
  ask the user.

## How to commit

- Stage only the files that belong to the current change (`git add
  <paths>`), not blanket `git add -A`, so unrelated edits don't sneak in.
  If you genuinely changed everything in the working tree on purpose,
  `-A` is fine — but check `git status` first.
- Use a short, imperative subject line (≤72 chars) describing the *why*
  or the user-visible effect, e.g.:
  - `Add custom HTML controls bar driven over mpv IPC`
  - `Fix mpv popup misalignment after DPI change`
  - `Refactor scan_videos to stream results`
- Add a body only if the change has non-obvious motivation or
  trade-offs. Wrap the body at ~72 chars.
- Pass multi-line messages via a heredoc so newlines survive PowerShell:

  ```powershell
  git commit -m "Subject line" -m "Body paragraph one." -m "Body paragraph two."
  ```

  Or use `git commit` with `-F` and a temp file if it's longer.
- **Do not** use `git commit --amend` to fold new work into a previous
  commit unless the user explicitly asks. Each agent step should be its
  own checkpoint.
- **Do not** push, force-push, rebase, or reset without an explicit
  request. Local commits are the checkpoint mechanism; the user
  controls history rewrites and remotes.

## After committing

Run `git status` to confirm the working tree is clean (or only contains
intentional leftovers), and mention the new commit's subject in your
reply so the user knows a checkpoint was created.

## Verifying before commit

When the change touches code (not just docs / config), run the relevant
type-checks first:

- Frontend (`src/`, `index.html`, `tsconfig*.json`, `vite.config.ts`):
  `npx tsc --noEmit`.
- Backend (`src-tauri/src/`, `Cargo.toml`):
  `cargo check --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml`.

If either fails, fix it before committing — don't checkpoint a broken
state.
